[Unit]
Description=Replay every idle ephor branch checkout onto its project's main branch
# After the refresh, so the pull-request reading the sweep asks for is usually
# current before it asks (§FS-004-quick-actions.6.1). Only ordering: a sweep
# that has to freshen a project's reading itself does so, and one that cannot
# have it replays none of that project's checkouts rather than guessing.
After=ephor-refresh.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# A checkout that sits for a day drifts far enough from main that work done in
# it is work against a layout that is gone, and nothing notices until `ship`,
# after the whole run has been paid for. This replays the ones nobody is
# holding, so a checkout somebody opens is usually current when they do.
#
# It passes over — and says it passed over — the project's main-branch checkout
# (`ephor update` owns that one), a checkout a live run holds, a branch with an
# open pull request that is not a draft, and a checkout an earlier sweep
# already wrote a conflict ticket about. A conflict here puts the tree back on
# the commit it was on and reports it: nobody is waiting on this replay, and a
# tree left mid-rebase by a timer at 03:00 is worse than the drift it was
# fixing (§FS-005-dispatch.12).
#
# `--act`: sweeping writes into trees, so `rebase` reports what it would do and
# writes nothing unless it is told to act, at every width it sweeps at
# (§FS-011-command-line.10). This unit is exactly the case the flag is for — a
# sweep with nobody watching — so it says so once here rather than being
# silently exempt. The reader's consent is at adopting this unit, not at each
# run.
#
# `--org` names the group to sweep. Change it to your own, or to `--workspace`
# or `--tag`; the verb takes one selector and nothing that names a single
# checkout (§FS-011-command-line.9).
ExecStart=%h/.cargo/bin/ephor rebase --org foundation --act
